FOR THE COORDINATOR — two one-line changes in files owned by the layered-arm stream.
Prepared by the surge-unlock branch, which is forbidden from touching src/taste/showdown.ts and
cli/beat.mjs's showdown sections. Neither hunk is applied on surge-unlock.

Both are guarded by tests that already exist on this branch:
  - test/surge-curation.test.ts  pins SURGE_ROLE_CATEGORIES_V2 (Pads out of chords, Chords/
    Polysynths/Sequences in) and asserts the two mappings agree on WHICH roles are surge-eligible.
  - test/surge-tempo.test.ts     proves a synced patch's modulation period tracks the tempo.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
HUNK 1 — src/taste/showdown.ts, the category mapping (research 141 §7.3)

Measured over the installed corpus (amp-EG attack of the sounding scene, 3,559 .fxp, re-verified
independently 2026-07-26): Pads median 537.8 ms / 18% <= 12.5 ms — 829.5 ms and 4.6% in the factory
pool alone — against 131's measured chords target of <= 12 ms. All 16 curated chords picks in the
old presets/surge-curated.json came from Pads; zero from Keys. Surge's own Chords (28 patches,
3.9 ms) and Polysynths (122, 3.9 ms) were never eligible.

--- a/src/taste/showdown.ts
+++ b/src/taste/showdown.ts
@@
-/** Role -> Surge factory patch-categories to draw a patch from. Pitched roles only; drum-loop
- * maps to null (skipped for v1 — see the section note). Matching is case-insensitive and by
- * substring so "Basses" also catches sub-categories like "Basses/Acid". */
-export const SURGE_ROLE_CATEGORIES: Record<string, readonly string[] | null> = {
-  bassline: ['Basses'],
-  chords: ['Pads', 'Keys'],
-  lead: ['Leads', 'Plucks'],
-  'drum-loop': null,
-}
+/** Role -> Surge patch-categories to draw a patch from. Pitched roles only; drum-loop maps to null
+ * (skipped for v1 — see the section note). Matching is case-insensitive and by substring so
+ * "Bass" also catches "Basses". The mapping itself lives in surgeCuration.ts so the curation
+ * script and the showdown draw share ONE definition — see SURGE_ROLE_CATEGORIES_V2 there for the
+ * measured justification (research 141 §7.3). */
+export const SURGE_ROLE_CATEGORIES: Record<string, readonly string[] | null> = SURGE_ROLE_CATEGORIES_V2

...and extend the existing import at line 80:

-import { curatedKey } from './surgeCuration.js'
+import { curatedKey, SURGE_ROLE_CATEGORIES_V2 } from './surgeCuration.js'

(surgeCuration.ts imports nothing from showdown.ts, so this does not create a cycle — showdown.ts
already imports curatedKey from it.)

If you prefer to keep the literal in showdown.ts rather than re-export, the exact replacement value
is:

  bassline: ['Basses', 'Bass'],
  chords: ['Chords', 'Polysynths', 'Synths', 'Keys'],
  lead: ['Leads', 'Plucks', 'Sequences', 'Arps', 'Rhythms'],
  'drum-loop': null,

...but then the two definitions can drift, which is the failure mode CLAUDE.md's parity rule names.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
HUNK 2 — cli/beat.mjs, the showdown surge render: pass the batch tempo (D6)

`batchBpm` is already in scope three lines above. Without this the showdown arm keeps rendering
every tempo-synced LFO/delay/arp at 120 BPM even though the sidecar now supports the tempo — and
because it is now Sequences-eligible (hunk 1), the mistiming would get MORE audible, not less.

--- a/cli/beat.mjs
+++ b/cli/beat.mjs
@@ (in showdownCmd, the ring-screen redraw loop, ~line 2862)
-                const res = await surgeMod.runSurgeRender({ patch: candidate.path, notes, sampleRate: 44100, outPath: attemptWav })
+                const res = await surgeMod.runSurgeRender({ patch: candidate.path, notes, sampleRate: 44100, outPath: attemptWav, tempo: batchBpm })

Optional but recommended alongside it: record `res.meta.tempoApplied` in the batch manifest so a
clip rendered on a build without the binding is visibly provenance-suspect rather than silently
mixed in with correct ones.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
PREREQUISITE for hunk 2 on any machine that renders surge

The tempo binding is a local patch to the surgepy source build:

  cd ~/Documents/dotbeat/tools/surge
  git apply <repo>/python/surge-patches/0001-surgepy-expose-host-tempo.patch
  cmake --build build --target surgepy          # and build-py310, if that is what the venv uses
  cp build-py310/src/surge-python/surgepy.cpython-310-darwin.so python/.venv/lib/python3.10/site-packages/

`node cli/beat.mjs showdown --surge-doctor` reports `tempoBinding: true` once it is in place. Until
then a render that names a tempo fails loudly (exit 4) rather than silently producing an
off-groove clip — which is the point.
